Alt text generation from images

Working with text is fairly straightforward, but let’s try something more complicated — images, and, more specifically, generating image descriptions.

Image alt text is important for making content accessible to visually impaired users and improving SEO, but it’s easy to overlook it when adding images on the web. Luckily, there is an easy way to automate it with Sanity AI Assist.

Let’s assume we have an image field. Inside its definition, we can nest a fields array and add our altText field. Then all we need to do is wire it up via imageDescriptionField in options.aiAssist:

defineField({
  type: 'image',
  name: 'eventImage',
  fields: [
    defineField({
      type: 'text',
      name: 'altText',
      title: 'Alternative text',
      rows: 2,
    }),
  ],
  options: {
    aiAssist: {
      imageDescriptionField: 'altText',
    },
  },
})
tsxCopy to clipboard

To see it in action, we can drag and drop an image, and its altText will be automatically generated once the image upload is complete. We can see the AI Assist indicator working next to the altText field, and, after it’s done, we can make any further tweaks if necessary.

Image generation from text

This was easy, but what if we don’t have an image? Maybe we are just prototyping something, waiting for our designer or illustrator to provide real images, or just want to play around with image generation? Returning to our bird example, let’s assume we have an image field with a nested text field for the image prompt. We can wire these up via the imageInstructionField property under options.aiAssist, similar to what we did for the text generation in the previous example:

