Finding a web-sized embedding model

We chose the gte-small model via @xenova/transformers for our embedding model. It strikes a nice balance for size, speed, and relevance features — and most importantly, it works well in a browser:

Capability: It has 384 dimensions and supports up to 512 max tokens per input. While this is less dimensions and context window than e.g., OpenAI’s text-embedding-ada-002 (1536 dimensions with 8192 max tokens), it is sufficient to produce semantically meaningful text chunks.
Size & speed: It’s a small, quick download at ~30mb and creating embedding results is fast (20-30ms in our demo case) and can compute via CPU or WebGPU.

Hooking this up to our posts content is straightforward:

import { pipeline } from "@xenova/transformers";

const extractor = await pipeline("feature-extraction", "Xenova/gte-small");
const text = "this is some text!";
const output = await extractor(text, { pooling: "mean", normalize: true });

console.log(Array.from(output.data));
// =>
[
   -0.04172841086983681, -0.0032770344987511635,   0.04789912328124046,
  ... 381 more items
]
