Where can we go from here?

At this point, we’ve walked through a powerful, flexible, and fast vector search app that runs entirely in the browser. But this is just a starting point that opens up many other interesting possibilities.

Hybrid search

Orama is a fully capable lexical search tool with many additional features — many of which can be combined with vector search. For starters, here’s a simple example that combines BM25 keyword relevance with semantic vector similarity, with a custom scoring balance.

const results = search(db, {
  mode: "hybrid",
  term: "Next.js", // Full-text search term
  vector: { value: queryEmbedding, property: "embeddings" },
  limit: 10,
  where: {
    date: { gte: dateToNumber("2023-01-01") },
  },
  // Adjust the balance between text and vector scoring
  hybridWeights: {
    text: 0.7,   // weight on BM25 keyword matching
    vector: 0.3, // weight on semantic similarity
  },
});
jsxCopy to clipboard

The ability to combine these different search features gives us powerful relevance options for everything from finding better results for users to powering better data context upstream to applications.

On-device AI applications

We’ve already fit an embedding model in the browser, and it turns out that generative models can fit as well. While Full-scale LLMs are still too large for most browsers, we can turn to the neat world of Small Language Models (”SLMs”) and tools. Projects like web-llm can run open weight SLMs in a browser with hardware acceleration. And, the Chrome browser continues to integrate advanced AI functionality, like the Prompt API that enables inference with a local copy of the Gemini Nano model, and with quite impressive outputs for such a small model.

It’s a natural step to pair our vector search journey here with a SLM to be able to build things like a fully browser-based retrieval-augmented generation (”RAG”) application or a vision capable application. The possibilities are endless with the combination of device inputs (voice, text, photos, videos) and use cases like offline functionality, fully private on-device data, and more. We look forward to exploring some of these ideas in future articles, so stay tuned!

LLM-as-a-judge for complex evaluations

Some things can't be scored with rules. Was the response helpful? Did the agent reason correctly? Was the tone appropriate? For these, you use an LLM as a judge: a separate model that scores your agent's output against defined criteria. This approach was formalised in the LLM-as-a-Judge paper by Zheng et al. and has become standard for evaluating open-ended outputs.

Mastra ships built-in LLM-based scorers for common evaluation needs, but you can also build custom ones. Here's a simple example attaching a relevancy scorer to your agent:

import { Agent } from "@mastra/core/agent";
import { openai } from "@ai-sdk/openai";
import {
  createAnswerRelevancyScorer,
  createFaithfulnessScorer,
  createToxicityScorer,
} from "@mastra/evals/scorers/llm";

const agent = new Agent({
  name: "support-agent",
  instructions: "You are a customer support agent...",
  model: openai("gpt-5"),
  scorers: {
    relevancy: {
      scorer: createAnswerRelevancyScorer({ model: openai("gpt-5-mini") }),
    },
  },
});
tsxCopy to clipboard

LLM-as-judge scorers themselves add cost and latency to your eval pipeline. Running five LLM-based scorers on every eval entry can get expensive quickly. This is another reason to combine them with cheap, fast code-based scorers, and to reserve LLM judges for the cases where deterministic scoring genuinely cannot capture what you need.

Integrating evals into the development workflow

If you only run evals manually before major releases, regressions slip through for weeks. There are two types of evaluations worth distinguishing:

Live evaluations score agent interactions in real time, running asynchronously on production traffic without blocking the response. Useful for continuous monitoring, but not the focus of this article.
Golden dataset evaluations run your agent against your curated test cases, either locally or in CI. This is where you catch regressions before they reach users.

In Mastra, this is built around the concept of experiments. You create a dataset, then run your agent against it with a set of scorers. Each run is a named experiment, making it easy to compare results across changes:

import { mastra } from "./index";
import { createAnswerRelevancyScorer } from "@mastra/evals/scorers/prebuilt";

const dataset = await mastra.datasets.get({ id: "support-agent-golden" });

const relevancy = createAnswerRelevancyScorer({ model: "openai/gpt-5-mini" });

const summary = await dataset.startExperiment({
  name: "improved-system-prompt-v2",
  targetType: "agent",
  targetId: "support-agent",
  scorers: [relevancy],
});

console.log(summary.status); // 'completed' | 'failed'
console.log(summary.succeededCount);
console.log(summary.failedCount);

for (const item of summary.results) {
  console.log(item.itemId, item.output);
  for (const score of item.scores) {
    console.log(`  ${score.scorerName}: ${score.score} - ${score.reason}`);
  }
}
tsxCopy to clipboard

Naming each experiment (like "improved-system-prompt-v2") lets you compare runs side by side and track whether a change actually moved scores in the right direction. A useful naming convention is "branchId-commitId", especially when running evals in CI.

Treat golden dataset evals like CI tests. A focused "regression" subset of your most critical test cases runs automatically on every PR that touches the agent (prompts, tools, or model changes). For bigger changes, run the full suite to verify nothing else broke.

How WebMCP works

WebMCP is an emerging standard that essentially allows web pages to be turned into an equivalent of Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers. The W3C Web Machine Learning Community Group leads the specification, with development championed by engineers at Google and Microsoft. In even the short time since the standard’s creation, it’s already seen a number of changes as the steering group iterates on practical, workable solutions.

At its core, WebMCP is a way for web applications to expose functionality in frontend JavaScript via MCP-like tools, so that agents can have understandable, schema-based, predictable interfaces to interact with a web app, while preserving the human interactive elements. It aims to fill a gap between existing MCP servers, which are backend-only, and frontend web automation (Chrome DevTools, Playwright, etc.) that controls a browser via a human-based UI. For a good introduction into when and why to reach for WebMCP, we suggest the Chrome team’s excellent post “When to use WebMCP and MCP.”

WebMCP works with both pure frontend applications, as we’ll discuss today, and traditional remote-backed web apps. For example, an e-commerce web application could expose everything from searchProducts to addToCart to checkout so that an agent (possibly even in combination with a human) could more quickly and easily navigate product discovery and purchase. Jack Herrington even has a quick demo video of him using a traditional chat tool to hypothetically order a sandwich from his browser.

These tools get the web applications’ logged-in auth and context, which allows powerful agent capabilities while piggybacking on the difficult things of authentication and personalization that a backend MCP tool would have more challenges with. To see the potential here, consider a company with extensive custom knowledge scattered across intranets and internal apps. (This was one of the original motivators of the MCP-B project.) Exposing and aggregating that functionality via WebMCP tools could be a faster, more direct path than writing dedicated MCP servers that require additional auth and access controls, as well as new backends.

Registering WebMCP tools

Getting started is pretty straightforward. A web application can share tools with navigator.modelContext.registerTool().

⚠️Browser requirements: If our demo apps don’t detect native WebMCP support, the functionality is automatically polyfilled. If you want true native WebMCP, you need Chrome 146+ and then set chrome://flags/#enable-webmcp-testing to Enabled.

The vector search functionality we’re interested in already exists as a searchPosts function (source). You can see the existing demo to get a sense of what’s available at nearform.github.io/vector-search-web. We pretty much just create an MCP-style schema around the searchPosts function with something like the following, and we have our first WebMCP tool:

navigator.modelContext.registerTool({
  name: "search_nearform_knowledge",
  description:
    "Vector search across Nearform blog posts and case studies using semantic similarity. Returns matching posts with titles, URLs, dates, and similarity scores.",
  inputSchema: {
    type: "object",
    properties: {
      query: {
        type: "string",
        description: "Search query (e.g. 'microservices architecture')",
      },
      postType: {
        type: "array",
        items: { type: "string", enum: POST_TYPE_VALUES },
        description: `Filter by type: ${POST_TYPE_VALUES.join(", ")}. [..]`,
      },
      minDate: {
        type: "string",
        description: "Only posts after this date, YYYY-MM-DD (optional)",
      },
      categoryPrimary: {
        type: "array",
        items: { type: "string", enum: CATEGORIES_LIST },
        description: `Filter by category: ${CATEGORIES_LIST.join(", ")} (optional)`,
      },
      maxChunks: {
        type: "number",
        description: "Maximum number of chunks to return (default 50, max 50)",
      },
    },
    required: ["query"],
  },
  annotations: { readOnlyHint: true },
  execute: async (args) => {
    const payload = /* SNIPPED - call and pass through searchPosts(args) */;
    return {
      content: [{ type: "text", text: JSON.stringify(payload, null, 2) }],
    };
  },
});
