If you’re Chrome DevTools-savvy, you can start working with the tool straight from the dev console. We additionally use the navigator.modelContextTesting built-in provided by Chrome’s WebMCP implementation, along with the WebMCP polyfills we put in our demo apps. It’s a nice tool for testing and debugging, especially since there aren’t many real WebMCP tool callers out there yet. You can navigate to nearform.github.io/vector-search-web, fire up the console, and you should be able to:

