Discovering and calling WebMCP tools from Claude Desktop with MCP-B

While WebMCP tool registration and management are part of the standard and are already available in browsers like Chrome, remote discovery and calling are entirely different stories at this point. There are no browser- or tool-native consumers of WebMCP yet, aside from the navigator.modelContextTesting helper we just saw in Chrome in the previous section.

That said, there are several useful intermediate tools and libraries. Perhaps the most useful project stepping into this consumer gap is MCP-B. Originally started before WebMCP, MCP-B provides many useful transports and bridges to enable WebMCP across various application scenarios.

The first transport we’ll discuss is @mcp-b/webmcp-local-relay. In a web application, after your WebMCP tool registration, you can add a pre-packaged snippet as follows:

<script src="https://unpkg.com/@mcp-b/webmcp-local-relay/dist/browser/embed.js"></script>
htmlCopy to clipboard

The snippet embeds an iframe that listens via websockets and relays WebMCP communication to a separate socket server.

Once this is configured, you can use the @mcp-b/webmcp-local-relay MCP server for discovery and calling. Any WebMCP tool in open browser apps with the embedded iframe snippet will be available. For example, as a Claude Desktop user, add this snippet to your mcpServers configuration:

"webmcp-local-relay": {
  "command": "npx",
  "args": ["-y", "@mcp-b/webmcp-local-relay@latest"]
}
jsxCopy to clipboard

With this, any discovered WebMCP tools will be available alongside any other MCP tools in Claude Desktop.

In the following example, we can see that webmcp-local-relay is using the search_nearform_knowledge tool from https://nearform.github.io/vector-search-web/ (open in a separate local browser window). For Claude Desktop users, we can click into the Result bubble to see the full tool call inputs and outputs. We get the full power of Claude chat models with our very localized WebMCP tools, contributing context.

The setup here is really useful: just configure @mcp-b/webmcp-local-relay once in Claude Desktop, then open any number of WebMCP-enabled web applications in a local browser and call their respective tools. This reduces the number of separate MCP tool configurations needed and addresses concerns such as authentication and personalization by using whatever an existing web application already uses and is willing to expose via WebMCP tools.

At this point, we’ve seen WebMCP tools called from the browser console and from Claude Desktop, both of which rely on external AI. But what if the AI itself also ran in the browser? Let’s push this web AI thing a bit further and build a full multi-agent system where all the functionality lives on-device.

On-device agents: powerful but constrained

Our demo features three agents (Coordinator, Researcher, Writer) who can research and respond to queries about Nearform’s content and expertise. To make things even more challenging, we continued our push with Web AI tools and crafted these agents using in-browser language models via the Chrome Prompt API. The model used is variable based on your browser and preferences. In my browser, at the Model Status tab in chrome://on-device-internals/, my M2 MacBook Air reports a backend type of GPU (highest quality), and Chrome has installed the v3Nano model (4GB in size). Among in-browser language models, the Chrome Prompt API is one of the most capable and flexible, but compared to familiar cloud-based foundation models, it’s an entirely different world.

While we’re not going to spend a ton of time on the Prompt API (we’ll definitely have in-depth posts on in-browser models in the future), there are some limitations that we need to tackle first to dig into our agents demo.

Handling really small context

The first limitation is that the Prompt API has an extremely limited context window. Here’s a simple way to see this in action:

const session = await LanguageModel.create();
const answer = await session.prompt("What is the capital of France?");
console.log(`Answer: ${answer}`);

// The context APIs are in transition (new / old)
const used = session.contextUsage ?? session.inputUsage;
const available = session.contextWindow ?? session.inputQuota;
console.log(`Context: ${used}/${available}`);
jsxCopy to clipboard

My machine reliably reports a total available context of around 9,000 tokens. This is practically nothing compared to the modern foundation model. Claude Sonnet and Opus just passed 1 million token context windows for general usage. But it’s still enough to do some useful work. For the demo, this limitation is one of the reasons that we chose to implement a multi-agent system. We have a dedicated Coordinator tasked solely with deciding which research or writing tasks to assign; the researcher uses context to make tool calls and generate research findings; and the writer then uses the compacted research findings to write the research notepad and formulate answers for the Coordinator to send to the user. We trade off time (this is much longer) and complexity (3 agents instead of 1) so that the context we need in the demo app is minimized, and we have more context available for things like the rest of the user conversation and more. But context was just one constraint. The other is that the model can’t actually call our tools... yet.

Actually doing a tool call

We glossed over the actual calls to our WebMCP tools earlier. And while it’s early days for calling WebMCP via foundation models, Chrome’s LanguageModel abstraction cannot do any tool calls yet!

Fortunately, the W3C steering group’s Prompt API proposal already has an accepted, detailed specification of tool calling, which will presumably land in Chrome in the future. In the meantime, developers have already developed simple polyfills for tool calling. The Web Agents demo includes future-ready detection of the tool feature and, for now, backstops by making a structured, separate LanguageModel call to obtain appropriate tool input parameters for a direct call to our WebMCP tools. For example, if we have the query “What is AI-native engineering and how does it change product development?” our Researcher agent will translate this to a tool call that looks like:

{
  "name": "search_nearform_knowledge",
  "args": {
    "query": "AI native engineering",
    "postType": [],
    "minDate": "2024-01-01",
    "categoryPrimary": [
      "ai",
      "product",
      "frontend",
      "backend",
      "devops"
    ]
  }
}
